{
  "requirement": "The Habeas Codicem project MUST keep its mdBook Markdown in `md/`, the `habeas` crate's Rust source in `src/`, and the `trust-but-verify` crate's Rust source in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/`. Its own book MUST use the Habeas preprocessor as specified by {cite}`requirements.cli.mdbook`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The structural obligations in `requirements.layout.project` are the directory locations and the preprocessor configuration. Each has a root or is covered by the default Cargo layout.\n\n- **Markdown in `md/`.** The alternative is `src = \"book\"` in `book.toml`, which builds the same book from another directory and breaks the clause. `book.toml#table[book]/pair[src]` sets `src = \"md\"`, so this root implements the clause.\n- **`habeas` crate source in `src/`.** The alternative is `[lib] path = \"lib/habeas.rs\"` or `[[bin]] path = \"bin/main.rs\"`, which behaves the same but moves the source. `Cargo.toml#table[lib]/pair[path]` (`src/lib.rs`) and `Cargo.toml#table[bin]/pair[path]` (`src/main.rs`) pin both targets under `src/`.\n- **`trust-but-verify` source in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/`.** `crates/trust-but-verify/Cargo.toml#table[package]` sets no `[lib]` path override. Cargo's default `src/lib.rs` therefore applies under the crate directory, and the source files live there. The location is set by the crate directory and Cargo's default target path, not by any marked configuration item. The workspace membership and path dependency in the root `Cargo.toml` already reference `crates/trust-but-verify` (`table[workspace]/pair[members]` and the `trust-but-verify` dependency). The sibling requirement `requirements.layout.project.workspace` covers that membership. No further configuration item exists that would need an {impl} marker here.\n- **Own book uses the Habeas preprocessor as in `requirements.cli.mdbook`.** The alternative is a `[preprocessor.habeas]` whose command is `habeas mdbook` or a direct `cargo run` line, which would not use the repository-local alias. `book.toml#table[preprocessor.habeas]/pair[command]` sets `cargo habeas mdbook`. The text cites `requirements.cli.mdbook` for the preprocessor details, and that root already carries the {impl} for both requirements. The alias in `.cargo/config.toml#table[alias]/pair[habeas]` belongs to `requirements.cli.mdbook`'s own obligations, not this one.\n\nThe four supplied roots cover every configurable structural obligation in the text.\n\nbehavioral: Obligations and the tests that cover them:\n\n1. Markdown is kept in `md/`. `tests/repository.rs#fn[project_book_uses_the_repository_local_preprocessor_alias]` asserts that `book.toml` has `book.src == \"md\"`. It also asserts that `md/SUMMARY.md` exists.\n\n2. The `habeas` crate's Rust source is kept in `src/`. `project_book_uses_the_repository_local_preprocessor_alias` asserts that `src/lib.rs` and `src/main.rs` exist. `tests/repository.rs#fn[crate_targets_are_declared_in_src]` asserts that `Cargo.toml` declares `lib.path == src/lib.rs`. It also asserts that there is exactly one bin, named `habeas`, with path `src/main.rs`.\n\n3. The `trust-but-verify` crate's source is kept in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/`. `tests/repository.rs#fn[workspace_contains_frontend_and_sdk_but_not_the_vendor_fork]` runs `cargo metadata`. It asserts that the workspace members are exactly `habeas` and `trust-but-verify`. It asserts that `crates/trust-but-verify/src/lib.rs` exists. It also asserts that the `trust-but-verify` dependency path is `crates/trust-but-verify`.\n\n4. The project's own book uses the Habeas preprocessor as `requirements.cli.mdbook` specifies. `project_book_uses_the_repository_local_preprocessor_alias` asserts that the `preprocessor.habeas` command is `cargo habeas mdbook`. It asserts that the renderers are `[\"habeas\"]`. It asserts that `.cargo/config.toml` has the alias `habeas = \"run --release -p habeas --\"`. This applies the cited requirement to this project's case. I did not reassess the cited requirement's own tests.\n\nThe three tests all carry `{test}` markers for `requirements.layout.project`. The text names no further observable obligation, such as a ban on Markdown or Rust files elsewhere. I found no gap.",
